`lib/Git/Libgit2/FFI.pm:450`:

    _attach git_stash_save => [ 'opaque*', 'git_repository', 'git_signature', 'string', 'uint' ] => 'int';

The C signature (`git2/stash.h:72-77`, identical in the bundled 1.9.3 headers under the Alien::Libgit2 share dir) is

    int git_stash_save(git_oid *out, git_repository *repo, const git_signature *stasher, const char *message, uint32_t flags);

`out` is a caller-allocated 20-byte `git_oid`, exactly like in `git_commit_create`, `git_tag_create` or `git_rebase_commit`, which this file binds correctly as `'opaque'`. With `'opaque*'` FFI::Platypus hands libgit2 the address of one pointer-sized temporary; libgit2 writes the 20-byte stash commit id there - 12 bytes past its end - and Perl gets the first 8 bytes of a SHA-1 back as an "address".

The POD (`FFI.pm:1914-1918`) compounds it: `git_stash_save(\my $stash, $repo, $sig, $msg, $flags)` - "Free with `git_object_free`". Following that calls `git_object_free` on eight hash bytes. `t/31-stash.t:50-53` asserts in the wrong direction (`ok( $stash, 'git_stash_save returned a non-NULL stash handle' )`), which is why the suite stays green.

Same defect class as k13/k14 (cherrypick/revert out-param), mirrored.

Done when: the binding is `[ 'opaque', ... ]`, the POD shows a 20-byte buffer plus `oid_to_hex` and drops the free advice, and `t/31-stash.t` asserts that the returned oid equals what `refs/stash` points at.

